===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|werre}}, from Late {{inh|en|ang|werre}}, {{m|ang|wyrre||armed conflict}}, from {{der|en|fro-nor|guerre|werre}} (compare modern {{cog|fr|guerre}}), from {{der|en|ML.|werra}}, from {{der|en|frk|*werru|t=confusion; quarrel}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*wers-|t=to mix up, confuse, beat, thresh}}. Gradually displaced native {{ncog|ang|beadu}}, {{m|ang|hild}}, {{m|ang|ġewinn}}, {{m|ang|orleġe}}, {{m|ang|wīġ}}, and many others as the general term for "war" during the Middle English period.

Related to {{cog|goh|werra||confusion, strife, quarrel}} and {{cog|de|verwirren|t=to confuse}}, but not to {{m|de|Wehr|t=defense}}. Also related to {{cog|osx|werran||to confuse, perplex}}, {{cog|nl|war||confusion, disarray}}, {{cog|fy|war|t=confusion}},
{{cog|ang|wyrsa|wyrsa, wiersa|worse}}, {{cog|non|verri||worse, orig. confounded, mixed up}}, {{cog|it|guerra||war}}. There may be a connection with {{m|en|worse}} and {{m|en|wurst}}.

